---
// "What we collect and why": the full notice behind "How we keep this fair" (CLAUDE.md non-negotiable 4,
// docs/research/legal.md). It describes what this build collects, and nothing it does not. When its substance changes,
// change NOTICE_VERSION in lib/apply.ts too: every application records the version its candidate was shown.
import CareersLayout from '../../layouts/CareersLayout.astro';
import { getPublicOrganization } from '../../lib/api';
import { NOTICE_VERSION } from '../../lib/apply';

const response = await getPublicOrganization(Astro.params.organization!);
if (response.status !== 200) {
  return new Response(null, { status: 404 });
}

const employer = response.data;
---

<CareersLayout
  title={`What we collect and why | ${employer.name}`}
  description={`How ${employer.name} and ScreenHire handle your information when you apply for a job.`}
  organizationName={employer.name}
  organizationSlug={employer.slug}
  branding={employer.branding}
>
  <article class="flex flex-col gap-6 text-[15px] leading-relaxed [&_h2]:font-display [&_h2]:text-xl [&_h2]:font-bold [&_li]:mt-1 [&_ul]:list-disc [&_ul]:pl-5">
    <header>
      <h1 class="font-display text-3xl leading-tight font-bold tracking-tight">What we collect and why</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 text-candidate-muted-foreground">
        {employer.name} uses ScreenHire to receive applications. {employer.name} decides how applications are used;
        ScreenHire handles them on its behalf.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="browsing">
      <h2 id="browsing">While you look at jobs</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Only our server logs: your network (IP) address, your browser's name, the page and the time. We use them to keep
        the site running and secure, and delete them within 30 days. There are no tracking cookies, no analytics and no
        device fingerprinting on these pages.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="applying">
      <h2 id="applying">When you apply</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Nothing is collected until you start on the form, below the notice that says what we check. A file goes to storage
        as soon as you choose it, and one that is never sent with an application is deleted after a day. With an
        application we keep:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>what you enter: your name, email, phone number, answers and the files you upload;</li>
        <li>the network (IP) address and browser you sent it from, and when you opened the form;</li>
        <li>
          counts of how you filled in the form, unless you switch on the assistive technology option: for each field, how
          many characters you typed and how many you pasted, how often you pasted, how many times you came back to it, how
          long you spent in it and whether your browser filled it in for you; and how many times you left the page;
        </li>
        <li>
          a device code, where the job allows it (in some countries only if you tick the box for it): your browser's
          settings, such as its language, screen size and time zone, turned into a one-way code;
        </li>
        <li>signs of automation software that your browser reports about itself;</li>
        <li>whether you switched on the assistive technology option;</li>
        <li>your answer about AI tools, and anything you tell us about how you used them;</li>
        <li>
          what our checks read from your files and answers: the text, any hidden text, the tool that made each file, a
          fingerprint of each written answer, and the result of any comparison;
        </li>
        <li>which version of this notice you were shown.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-2">
        We delete the network address, browser, form timing, the counts and the device code within 30 days, and the counts
        from a form you never send within a day. What we keep from them is coarse: the network's operator and country,
        never your address or a location more precise than a country.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="after">
      <h2 id="after">After you apply</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        {employer.name} may email you a link to a few more questions. The link works for 14 days and only for you. That
        page keeps your answers and nothing else: no counts of how you typed, no device code and no checks of your network.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="future-roles">
      <h2 id="future-roles">Future roles</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        At the end of the form you can tick "Keep my details for future roles". It is up to you, and it is never ticked for
        you. If you tick it, {employer.name} keeps your application for the number of months the form shows, and may email
        you an invitation to apply for another role. Every invitation has a link that stops this at once. When the time
        runs out, or you stop it, {employer.name} no longer keeps your details for future roles or invites you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="checks">
      <h2 id="checks">What we check, and what we never do</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        We check applications for signs of bots and impersonation, because they crowd out real candidates. The form has a
        hidden field that only automated software fills in, and the site may ask your browser to complete a bot challenge
        from Cloudflare, which then sees your network address and browser. Files are checked to be the type they say they
        are.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        We also look at how the application arrived. We check whether the network belongs to a hosting provider, a VPN or
        Tor, and whether its country matches your phone number's. We check whether your email domain is a throwaway service
        or cannot receive mail, and whether your phone number is valid. We note if the form was finished faster than a
        person could. And we check whether the same email, phone number, file or device code appears on other applications
        to {employer.name}. The device code is made differently for every employer, so it never matches across them. Each
        check is one piece of evidence. None of them is a judgement on its own.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        The counts of how the form was filled in show, for example, that most of a long answer was pasted, or that you
        left the page and came back to one answer many times. They never raise a concern on their own, because dictation,
        screen readers and autofill look the same. We never record what you type, which keys you press or the time between
        them, and we never see what you copied or where you went when you left the page.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        We read the files you upload and your written answers. In a file, we look for text people cannot see, such as
        white or very small text, and for what the file says about the tool that made it. We check whether a written
        answer is nearly identical to another applicant's answer to {employer.name} from the last 180 days.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-2">
        If {employer.name} has turned it on, an AI model from our provider compares the facts in your resume, your answers
        and any LinkedIn profile export you upload, such as job titles, dates and qualifications, and shows where they
        differ by quoting both. It is never asked whether you used AI to write them. Our provider is not allowed to use
        your information to train its models. A person decides what any difference means, and there is often an ordinary
        explanation.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-2">
        <li>Our checks for bots and impersonation never reject you. A person at {employer.name} looks at anything they flag.</li>
        <li>We never give you a score or a percentage, and never guess at your feelings, honesty or personality.</li>
        <li>We never look at your other tabs or apps, and never use your camera or microphone.</li>
        <li>We never look up your social media profiles. We only use what you give us.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="assistive">
      <h2 id="assistive">Assistive technology</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Dictation, screen readers, switch access and autofill can look like copying and pasting. If you use them, switch
        on the assistive technology option at the start of the form. We then turn off any checks on how you type or paste,
        and on how long the form took.
        Using the option is never held against you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="ai">
      <h2 id="ai">AI tools</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        Each job says whether {employer.name} welcomes AI help with your application. We ask you about it at the end of
        the form, and a person reads your answer alongside your application.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="rights">
      <h2 id="rights">Your choices</h2>
      <p class="mt-2">
        You can ask {employer.name} to see, correct or delete your information, and to have a person review anything we
        flagged about your application. Contact them through the email address they use to write to you.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="font-mono text-[11px] text-candidate-muted-foreground">NOTICE VERSION {NOTICE_VERSION}</p>
  </article>
</CareersLayout>
